          "question": "Is there a case where two conditions documented together must NOT be linked by the coder?",
          "answer": "In coding, there are specific cases where certain conditions documented together must not be linked by the coder. One of the key rules to remember is when codes are designated as \"Excludes1.\" This means the two conditions cannot both be present, and the codes are never reported together. This is established as a strong condition in the coding guidelines (source: \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\").\n\nAdditionally, in the case of asthma, \"exacerbation\" and \"status asthmaticus\" are alternatives where only one can be coded at a time; documenting both is not permitted (source: \"Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes\").\n\nI could not find additional specific scenarios beyond these examples from the corpus. If you have further details or specific conditions in mind, please provide them for more focused information.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
